> \begin{proposition}[Limits of the monadic reading]\label{prop:monad-not}
> \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
> \item $D\cdot\operatorname{End}(f)=\{a\in A_f:a\ge0,\ s(a)=1\}=\Cpl(f,f)$ is a proper subset of $A_f$ that spans it. It is a
>   convex set closed under gluing, not a subspace.
> \item The two-sided ideals of $A_f$ are exactly $0$, $\R P_f$, $I_f$ and $A_f$, and every nonzero circulation generates $I_f$. So the
>   maximal chains of ideals are $0\subsetneq\R P_f\subsetneq A_f$ and $0\subsetneq I_f\subsetneq A_f$, and there is no chain
>   $0\subsetneq J_1\subsetneq J_2\subsetneq A_f$.
> \item $HH^n(A_f,M)=0$ for every $A_f$-bimodule $M$ and every $n\ge1$. For a mass-preserving linear $F_1\colon A_f\to A_{f'}$, the rule
>   $a\cdot x=F_1(a)\cdot x$ makes $I_{f'}$ a left $A_f$-module if and only if $b_2=0$.
> \end{enumerate}
> \end{proposition}
> \begin{proof}
> (i) is \cref{prop:kleisli}(i); the set is not closed under negation. (ii) By \cref{thm:structure}(iii), $A_f\cong\R\times M_{n-1}(\R)$,
> a product of two simple algebras, whose two-sided ideals are the four products of $0$ or the whole factor. A nonzero $x\in I_f$
> lies in the simple factor $I_f$, so it generates $I_f$. (iii) $A_f$ is a product of matrix algebras over $\R$, hence separable, so
> it is projective as a bimodule over itself and its Hochschild cohomology vanishes in positive degrees. The module axiom
> $F_1(ab)\cdot x=F_1(a)\cdot(F_1(b)\cdot x)$ says $b_2(a,b)\cdot x=0$ for all $x\in I_{f'}$; taking $x=e_{f'}$, the unit of $I_{f'}$, gives
> $b_2(a,b)=0$, since $b_2(a,b)\in I_{f'}$.
> \end{proof}
> 
> So $b_2$ satisfies the Hochschild cocycle identity only formally (the arity-three equation of \cref{thm:forced}): there is no
> bimodule in which it is a cocycle unless it vanishes, and no Hochschild class could carry it anyway.
